ct a structured roleplay, record evidence and assumptions, compare implementation alternatives, and produce a one-page brief. A real interview is optional and requires authorization from the people involved; the learning project can be completed entirely with fictional data.\",\n    \"deliverables\": [\n      \"A discovery-question plan and notes from two roleplays or authorized interviews.\",\n      \"A one-page problem, scope, constraints, and success brief.\",\n      \"A baseline-measurement and evaluation plan.\",\n      \"An alternatives comparison and a risk/owner list.\"\n    ],\n    \"acceptance\": [\n      \"The brief identifies the user and a concrete current workflow.\",\n      \"Measured facts, interview estimates, and unknowns are distinguished.\",\n      \"In-scope and excluded tasks are explicit.\",\n      \"Data access, constraints, and operating ownership are recorded.\",\n      \"Success criteria include a baseline and an evaluation method.\"\n    ],\n    \"starter\": \"lesson-26-starter.zip\",\n    \"filename\": \"discovery-notes.md\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 27,\n    \"title\": \"An evidence-based portfolio case study\",\n    \"brief\": \"Choose one completed learning project and package it for a reader who has no context. Write a concise case study, verify the repository and demo links, include a representative test report, and explain your own contribution. Use the app's portfolio tracker to keep the artifacts together and export a readable copy.\",\n    \"deliverables\": [\n      \"A problem-first case study with scope and contribution.\",\n      \"Verified project links or a clearly documented local demo.\",\n      \"A representative test/evaluation record and actual result.\",\n      \"A small implementation diagram and explicit limits.\"\n    ],\n    \"acceptance\": [\n      \"The case study states a specific user problem and resulting behavior.\",\n      \"Your implementation and decision contribution are clear.\",\n      \"Outcome claims link to evidence or are labelled estimates.\",\n      \"Demo/repository setup or local-delivery instructions are verified.\",\n      \"The architecture, limitations, and next step match the actual project.\"\n    ],\n    \"starter\": \"lesson-27-starter.zip\",\n    \"filename\": \"case-study.md\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 28,\n    \"title\": \"Customer-policy assistant capstone\",\n    \"brief\": \"Build a fictional customer's read-only policy assistant using a clear interface, trusted backend boundary, source-grounded answer flow, feedback storage, and an evaluation suite. Start with a labelled offline mode if necessary, then add a real provider only when its contract and secret handling are understood. Keep external messaging, purchases, and autonomous writes outside this capstone scope.\",\n    \"deliverables\": [\n      \"A scoped customer brief and actual architecture diagram.\",\n      \"A working question-to-evidence-to-answer workflow.\",\n      \"Feedback persistence and clear product failure states.\",\n      \"Acceptance evidence, a repeatable eval suite, and an unfinished-work list.\"\n    ],\n    \"acceptance\": [\n      \"The capstone follows a specific brief with explicit excluded actions.\",\n      \"A complete user question produces supported evidence and a usable response.\",\n      \"Missing evidence and service failure have honest distinct states.\",\n      \"Access policy and feedback persistence are tested at their boundaries.\",\n      \"A repeatable eval report and remaining-work list accompany the implementation.\"\n    ],\n    \"starter\": \"lesson-28-starter.zip\",\n    \"filename\": \"capstone_acceptance.py\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 29,\n    \"title\": \"A hardened capstone release package\",\n    \"brief\": \"Take the capstone through a documented release-readiness process. Verify its actual environment, permissions, bounded failures, stored feedback, and operating limits. Rehearse recovery in an isolated setup using fictional data. Produce a release decision with evidence and unresolved checks instead of claiming universal production readiness.\",\n    \"deliverables\": [\n      \"A versioned hosted or reproducible release record.\",\n      \"Permission, failure, resource, and data check evidence.\",\n      \"A recovery runbook and isolated rehearsal result.\",\n      \"An operational-owner map and explicit release limitations.\"\n    ],\n    \"acceptance\": [\n      \"The tested release and environment are explicitly identified.\",\n      \"Allowed and forbidden access paths are verified after deployment.\",\n      \"Timeout, provider failure, and budget exhaustion are bounded and visible.\",\n      \"Data durability and recovery compatibility are checked in an isolated rehearsal.\",\n      \"Configuration, alerts, backup, and escalation have documented owners.\"\n    ],\n    \"starter\": \"lesson-29-starter.zip\",\n    \"filename\": \"release-readiness.md\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"day\": 30,\n    \"title\": \"A customer-ready demo and handoff pack\",\n    \"brief\": \"Package your capstone as a scoped delivery. Record a concise demo, assemble acceptance and evaluation evidence, verify the operating instructions with another person, and write a retrospective. Produce a next-project plan tied to one demonstrated weakness. Do not publish private customer data or send promotional messages as part of this learning exercise.\",\n    \"deliverables\": [\n      \"A short demo script or recording tied to actual acceptance criteria.\",\n      \"A versioned delivery pack with operating and recovery instructions.\",\n      \"A handoff walkthrough observation and a corrected documentation gap.\",\n      \"A retrospective and a concrete follow-up learning project.\"\n    ],\n    \"acceptance\": [\n      \"The demo shows a normal workflow and a meaningful failure boundary.\",\n      \"Claims identify the actual release, data scope, and execution mode.\",\n      \"Acceptance and evaluation evidence are linked in the 